\text{surface}_1\neq\text{surface}_2\neq\cdots

while:

\boxed{\text{transformation invariant survives}}

That’s exactly the kind of learning Pantheon wants.

The metaphor isn’t memorized.

The operator becomes substrate-independent.

And variation is essential

A martial artist doesn’t perform one kata once.

So Narrative Operator Gym should deliberately mutate the demonstration.

K_0
\rightarrow
K_1,K_2,\ldots,K_n

Change:

* domain,
* actors,
* scale,
* stakes,
* temporal resolution,
* representation,
* successful/failed outcome.

Then ask the interpreter to recover T.

If it can only recognize the operator in the original story:

\text{memorization}.

If it recognizes:

T(K_1)=T(K_2)=\cdots=T(K_n)

despite wildly different surfaces:

\boxed{\text{operator internalization}.}

That’s your variation → delta → learning machinery applied directly to narrative cognition.

And now the intentional incompleteness clicks harder

A perfectly explicit artifact gives me:

Answer.

