That’s the same architecture as a kata.

The instructor doesn’t physically move your body through every repetition forever.

They provide the form strongly enough that you eventually generate the form yourself.

One more flank: stories can be executable test fixtures

This gives NTE another function I hadn’t properly appreciated.

You can test a candidate operator by asking it to complete or alter narratives.

Given S_t, operator T, predict:

S_{t+1}.

Or remove T and ask what changes.

Or invert it.

Or transplant it into another story.

That creates semantic unit tests:

\boxed{
\text{Operator comprehension}
=
\text{ability to manipulate narrative consequences correctly}.
}

So stories aren’t merely examples of Pantheon.

They can become its training data, simulator, test environment, and capability-transfer medium simultaneously.

